# AWS CodeDeploy: Implementing the Full CI/CD Pipeline on AWS

## What I Learned Today

Today I completed the CI/CD pipeline by adding the **Continuous Delivery (CD)** stage using **AWS CodeDeploy**. This session covers deploying a Docker-containerized Flask application onto an EC2 instance, then wiring everything together through **AWS CodePipeline** for full end-to-end automation.

> **Prerequisite:** This session builds directly on Day 14. The CI pipeline (GitHub → CodePipeline → CodeBuild → Docker Hub) must already be working before adding CodeDeploy.

---

## Complete CI/CD Flow (End-to-End)

```
Developer pushes code to GitHub
          ↓
AWS CodePipeline detects the change
          ↓
Stage 1 — Source:   GitHub (fetches latest code + appspec.yml)
          ↓
Stage 2 — Build:    AWS CodeBuild (builds & pushes Docker image to Docker Hub)
          ↓
Stage 3 — Deploy:   AWS CodeDeploy
                      → Runs scripts/stopcontainer.sh  (stop old container)
                      → Runs scripts/startcontainer.sh (pull & run new container)
                      → Deploys to EC2 instance
```

---

## New Files Added to the Repository

```
day-14/simple-python-app/
├── app.py
├── requirements.txt
├── Dockerfile
├── buildspec.yml
├── appspec.yml          ← NEW: CodeDeploy configuration (must be at repo root)
└── scripts/
    ├── stopcontainer.sh  ← NEW: Stops the running Docker container
    └── startcontainer.sh ← NEW: Pulls latest image and starts the container
```

> ⚠️ `appspec.yml` **must be placed at the root of the GitHub repository**. Unlike `buildspec.yml`, CodeDeploy does not allow a custom path for this file when using GitHub as the source.

---

## Step 1 — Create an EC2 Instance

1. Go to **EC2 → Launch Instance**.
2. Name: `sample-python-app`
3. OS: Ubuntu, Instance type: `t2.micro`
4. Use an existing key pair.
5. Network: default VPC, **enable Auto-assign Public IP**.
6. Security group: allow **SSH (port 22)**. Port 5000 can be opened later for app access.

### Add a Tag to the EC2 Instance

Tags allow CodeDeploy to identify which EC2 instances to deploy to.

- **Key:** `Name`
- **Value:** `sample-python`

> CodeDeploy uses these tags to target one or many EC2 instances. All instances sharing the same tag key-value pair will receive the deployment.

---

## Step 2 — Install the CodeDeploy Agent on EC2

SSH into the EC2 instance and run the following commands:

```bash
# Update packages
sudo apt update

# Install required dependencies
sudo apt install ruby-full -y
sudo apt install wget -y

# Download the CodeDeploy agent installer (replace region as needed)
wget https://aws-codedeploy-us-east-1.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/latest/install

# Grant execute permissions
chmod +x ./install

# Install the agent
sudo ./install auto

# Check agent status
sudo service codedeploy-agent status
```

If the agent is not running, start it manually:

```bash
sudo service codedeploy-agent start
sudo service codedeploy-agent status
```

> The bucket name in the `wget` URL depends on the AWS region where the EC2 instance was created. Refer to the [AWS CodeDeploy agent installation documentation](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/codedeploy/latest/userguide/codedeploy-agent-operations-install-ubuntu.html) to get the correct bucket name for your region.

### Install Docker on EC2

```bash
sudo apt install docker.io -y
```

---

## Step 3 — Create IAM Roles

Two IAM roles are needed:

### 3.1 EC2 Instance Role (allows EC2 to communicate with CodeDeploy)

1. Go to **IAM → Roles → Create role**.
2. Trusted entity: **AWS service → EC2**.
3. Attach policy: `AmazonEC2FullAccess` (or a scoped policy for CodeDeploy access).
4. Name: `ec2-codedeploy-role`.

**Attach the role to the EC2 instance:**
- EC2 Console → select instance → **Actions → Security → Modify IAM role** → select `ec2-codedeploy-role`.

**Restart the CodeDeploy agent after attaching the role:**

```bash
sudo service codedeploy-agent restart
sudo service codedeploy-agent status
```

### 3.2 CodeDeploy Service Role (allows CodeDeploy to call AWS services)

1. Go to **IAM → Roles → Create role**.
2. Trusted entity: **AWS service → CodeDeploy**.
3. Attach policy: `AWSCodeDeployRole`.
4. Name: `codedeploy-service-role`.

---

## Step 4 — Create the CodeDeploy Application

1. Go to **CodeDeploy → Applications → Create application**.
2. **Application name:** `sample-python-flask-app`
3. **Compute platform:** EC2/On-premises
4. Click **Create application**.

---

## Step 5 — Create a Deployment Group

A deployment group links the CodeDeploy application to the target EC2 instances.

1. Inside the application, click **Create deployment group**.
2. **Deployment group name:** `sample-python-app`
3. **Service role:** select `codedeploy-service-role`
4. **Deployment type:** In-place
5. **Environment configuration:** Amazon EC2 instances
- **Key:** `Name` | **Value:** `sample-python`
- CodeDeploy will immediately show how many matching instances were found.
6. **Agent configuration:** CodeDeploy agent is already installed — no changes needed.
7. **Load balancer:** disable (not required for this demo).
8. Click **Create deployment group**.

---

## Step 6 — Write the appspec.yml

This file tells CodeDeploy what scripts to run and when. It must be at the **repository root**.

```yaml
version: 0.0
os: linux
hooks:
  ApplicationStop:
- location: scripts/stopcontainer.sh
      timeout: 300
      runas: root
  AfterInstall:
- location: scripts/startcontainer.sh
      timeout: 300
      runas: root
```

---

## Step 7 — Write the Deployment Scripts

### scripts/stopcontainer.sh

Stops and removes any currently running Docker container before deploying the new one.

```bash
#!/bin/bash

# Get the container ID of the running container
CONTAINER_ID=$(sudo docker ps | awk '{if(NR>1) print $1}')

# Force remove if a container is running
if [ -n "$CONTAINER_ID" ]; then
  sudo docker rm -f $CONTAINER_ID
fi
```

### scripts/startcontainer.sh

Pulls the latest Docker image from Docker Hub and starts it.

```bash
#!/bin/bash

# Pull the latest image
sudo docker pull <your-dockerhub-username>/simple-python-flask-app:latest

# Run the container
sudo docker run -d -p 5000:5000 <your-dockerhub-username>/simple-python-flask-app:latest
```

> Replace `<your-dockerhub-username>` with your actual Docker Hub username.

---

## Step 8 — Test CodeDeploy Manually (Before Wiring to CodePipeline)

1. Go to **CodeDeploy → Applications → sample-python-flask-app → Deployments**.
2. Click **Create deployment**.
3. **Deployment group:** `sample-python-app`
4. **Revision type:** My application is stored in GitHub
5. Connect to GitHub (provide OAuth or Personal Access Token on first use).
6. **Repository name:** `<github-username>/<repo-name>`
7. **Commit ID:** paste the latest commit ID from GitHub.
8. Click **Create deployment**.

### Verify Deployment

```bash
# On the EC2 instance — check running containers
sudo docker ps

# Check pulled images
sudo docker images
```

If the Flask app container is running on port 5000, the deployment was successful.

---

## Step 9 — Add CodeDeploy Stage to CodePipeline

Now wire CodeDeploy into the existing CodePipeline to automate the full CI/CD flow.

1. Go to **CodePipeline → select the existing pipeline → Edit**.
2. Click **Add stage** after the Build stage.
3. **Stage name:** `Deploy`
4. Click **Add action group**:
- **Action name:** `CodeDeploy`
- **Action provider:** AWS CodeDeploy
- **Input artifacts:** `BuildArtifact` (output from CodeBuild stage)
- **Application name:** `sample-python-flask-app`
- **Deployment group:** `sample-python-app`
5. Click **Done → Save**.

---

## Step 10 — Verify the Full End-to-End Pipeline

1. Make any small change to the GitHub repository (e.g., add a blank line to `app.py`).
2. Commit and push.
3. Watch **CodePipeline** — all three stages (Source → Build → Deploy) should go **In Progress** and then **Succeeded**.
4. Verify on the EC2 instance:

```bash
sudo docker ps
```

The container should be running with an updated image timestamp.

To access the Flask app from a browser, open **port 5000** in the EC2 security group inbound rules and visit:

```
http://<ec2-public-ip>:5000
```

---

## Common Errors & Fixes

| Error | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `Docker: command not found` during deployment | Docker not installed on EC2 | Run `sudo apt install docker.io -y` on the EC2 instance |
| `Bind for 0.0.0.0:5000 failed: port already allocated` | Old container still running; `stopcontainer.sh` not properly configured | Implement `stopcontainer.sh` to remove the running container before deploying |
| `appspec.yml not found` | File not placed at root of GitHub repository | Move `appspec.yml` to the repo root (not inside a subfolder) |
| CodeDeploy agent not communicating | IAM role not attached to EC2 or agent not restarted after role attachment | Attach the IAM role and restart: `sudo service codedeploy-agent restart` |
| `Requested access denied` on Docker push | Docker not logged in during CodeBuild | Ensure `docker login` is in the `pre_build` phase of `buildspec.yml` |

---

## AWS Tags — Why They Matter

Tags are key-value pairs attached to AWS resources. I learned that in real-world usage, tags serve two purposes:

- **CodeDeploy targeting** — identifies which EC2 instances belong to a deployment group.
- **Cost tracking & governance** — filter all AWS resources by team, project, or environment. Example tags:
- `project: payments`
- `environment: production`
- `team: backend`

Management can query all resources under a specific tag to track usage and costs without manually checking each service.

---

## Summary — Complete AWS CI/CD Stack

| Stage | AWS Service | Role |
|---|---|---|
| Source | GitHub / AWS CodeCommit | Hosts source code; triggers pipeline on push |
| Orchestration | AWS CodePipeline | Detects changes; invokes Build and Deploy stages |
| Build | AWS CodeBuild | Runs `buildspec.yml`; builds and pushes Docker image |
| Deploy | AWS CodeDeploy | Runs `appspec.yml` hooks; pulls image and starts container on EC2 |
| Secret Storage | AWS SSM Parameter Store | Stores Docker credentials securely |
| Compute Target | Amazon EC2 | Hosts the running application container |

---
